Our research investigates the implications of the J/ψ particle decaying into invisible matter for light sterile neutrinos and sub-GeV dark matter. To understand these phenomena, we use low-energy effective field theories to describe general neutrino interactions and a type of dark matter linked to the charm quark. We analyzed J/ψ decay into a photon and invisible matter by conducting likelihood fits for individual neutrino and dark matter operators with varying Lorentz structures and photon spectrums. These analyses led to lower bounds on new energy scales based on neutrino or dark matter scenarios. Specifically, the most stringent limits on energy scales were found to be 12.8 GeV for neutrinos and 11.6 GeV for dark matter within their respective effective field theories. We also found that J/ψ decay into purely invisible matter imposes additional constraints on certain effective operators, with energy scales above 100 GeV for dipole operators. Moreover, our analysis revealed that J/ψ invisible decays can be sensitive to light dark matter mass ranges inaccessible to direct detection experiments, with future facilities such as the Super Tau Charm Factory having the potential to significantly lower these limits by two orders of magnitude. Our study suggests that J/ψ invisible decays into a photon are either already being investigated or have the potential to be probed by existing or future experiments with 10-100 GeV energy scales, and that further research is necessary to accurately determine these limits. Our analysis is applicable to J/ψ invisible decays into other light quarks, such as the u or s quarks, with slightly different energy scale limits due to different J/ψ production processes. We also discussed the relationships between our effective field theory approach and certain Standard Model Extensions, as well as the potential for J/ψ invisible decays to provide additional evidence for certain physics phenomena. Our research demonstrates that J/ψ invisible decays offer promising avenues for discovering light sterile neutrinos and a type of dark matter linked to the charm quark. Our study also highlights the potential for further research in this field, given the limited understanding of J/ψ invisible decays beyond the effective theories used here. Our results for the energy scale limits have been made available in an accompanying notebook that can be utilized to explore this research further. We emphasized the importance of experimental investigations into J/ψ invisible decays, particularly for the $\overline{c}\gamma
